WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:01.500
All right.

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.990
With that, we're about
three minutes past 7:00.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.370
So I think I'm going
to get started.

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:07.960
Thank you, everyone, for coming.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.270
I'm Shannon Rose McAuliffe.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.400
And I'm the manager of
the Student Arts Programs

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.750
here at MIT.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.750
We're very pleased to hear from
this really fantastic panel

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.260
of students and faculty
involved in the Arts at MIT.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.560
Joining us, we have Justin
Park from SyncopAsian,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.560
Ryan Kim and Junhee Li
from the Logarhythms,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.560
and MTA Professor Ian Hattwick.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:29.975
Thank you all for being here.

00:00:29.975 --> 00:00:32.100
Tonight, this group is
going to share with us a bit

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.040
about their experiences
with playing and recording

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.350
virtual music
during the pandemic.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.380
And then, we'll have some time
for questions and comments.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.810
And before we get
underway, quick

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.300
disclaimer that this
event is being recorded

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.100
and may be made
publicly available

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.730
on the Arts on the Radar website
or elsewhere in the future.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.220
If you do not wish to
appear on the recording,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.440
please make sure that
your video and audio

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.270
are muted during the event.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:01.090
And please contact us at
Arts on the Radar at MIT

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.260
dot edu if you've got any
questions or concerns.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.470
And with that, I'm going to
turn it over to the Logarhythms.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:07.980
Hello.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:08.820
My name is Junhee.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.310
I'm the President
of the MIT Logs.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:14.070
We're an a capella
group on campus.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.720
We have been around
for 70 years.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.840
This is our 71st or 72nd
year performing, I think.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.250
So we're a pretty old group.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:29.690
And by nature of a capella,
primarily what we do is sing.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:32.210
So in a normal year,
that would mean

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.760
that we spend around
six hours a week

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:39.200
separated into three two-hour
rehearsals rehearsing music.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.530
So that, of course,
in general requires

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:46.070
a lot of physical connection.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.290
We're all in the
same studio singing

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:52.190
to each other reactively,
adjusting volume, pitch,

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:53.670
so on, and so forth.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:57.440
And as you can expect, a lot
of the music making in general

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.190
is very collaborative
and in person.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:05.600
So when we were sent
home last semester,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.130
there was a big
transition period

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:08.330
during which we
were figuring out

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.474
how we would proceed with
the rest of the semester.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.610
Fortunately for us, we had
a lot of time to think.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:18.830
But also, we had a
lot of stuff kind

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:20.750
of taken care of beforehand.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.390
So we were in a
situation where we

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.730
had a lot of
arrangements figured out.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.238
We were in a situation
where everything

00:02:28.238 --> 00:02:29.780
that we had planned
for this semester

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.100
was pretty much already done.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:35.200
And it was just a
matter of finding a way

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.580
to perform it somehow.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:39.190
So that made the transition
process much easier.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.580
But, ultimately,
what we ended up

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:46.593
doing is we had a weekly
Zoom meeting during which,

00:02:46.593 --> 00:02:48.510
most of the time, we
actually didn't rehearse.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.770
Most of the time, it was
actually just social.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.410
But simultaneously,
there was a lot

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.830
of time spent going over
old arrangements, sometimes

00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:00.540
new arrangements, annotating and
thinking about what we wanted

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.720
to sound like, how we
should sing our parts,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.160
and also listening
to the music we

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.720
were supposed to be learning.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.760
So kind of the
individual processes

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.190
that we would normally do
to prepare for a rehearsal,

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:14.560
we just kind of did
it together on Zoom.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.950
And that was kind of how we
replaced in-person auditory

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.327
rehearsals.

00:03:20.327 --> 00:03:21.910
And then, simultaneously,
we had a lot

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.580
of music prepared that
we wanted to share.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.710
So we really, really wanted
to find a way to present that.

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:32.650
So our primary inspiration
was through a bunch

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:34.900
of other musicians who've
already been doing this even

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.430
without the pandemic.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:40.150
For instance, Eric Whittaker
had a concert, a virtual choir

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.910
concert, that was
very, very popular.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:43.810
Had a lot of singers.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:45.580
And also artists
like Jacob Collier

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:49.660
have done lots and lots of
collaborative videos, merged

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:50.620
videos and audio.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:52.370
And so, we thought it
would be a good idea

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.810
to produce videos that
were produced separately,

00:03:55.810 --> 00:04:00.130
recorded audio and video in
remote distant locations,

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:04.370
and then combined and produced
into one final product.

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:09.000
And then we also had an
idea to queue our videos,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.710
stockpile them, and
then release them

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:14.670
all in kind of a concert style
at the end of the semester.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:18.450
So that was the framework for
how our semester went down.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:22.320
Overall, we had a pretty
productive semester.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.900
The amount of music
learning that we did

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.820
and the amount of music
making that we did

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:29.790
was actually not very different
from what we would normally

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.010
do in a semester.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.880
So I think that what we did
was a pretty good replacement,

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.600
at least for our
way of functioning.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.750
And also the Twitch stream
at the end of the semester

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.670
was also surprisingly
more successful

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.030
than we thought it would be.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:49.770
And it was a lot of fun to
get to kind of watch that

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.950
come together and see
the audience enjoying it.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:58.290
Although there is, of course, no
auditory or in-person reaction.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:04:59.910
But it was cool
to see that there

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:01.868
were a lot of people
watching, giving comments,

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:04.320
and stuff like that.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.060
As for the more
technical aspects

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:08.130
of what we did
over the semester,

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.920
I'm going to hand
that over to Ryan.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:13.470
Ryan was our, Ryan is
our event coordinator.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.440
And he was in charge
of most of the concert

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.440
that we produced at the
end of last semester.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:20.517
So yeah.

00:05:20.517 --> 00:05:21.600
I'll turn it over to Ryan.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.200
And he can talk more about that.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:23.700
Yeah.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:24.810
Thanks, Junhee.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.590
Great to be here with you all.

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:32.330
So, as Junhee said, we did some
remote a capella production

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:34.520
this past semester
because we weren't

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.530
able to be physically
on campus together.

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:39.110
We found a way to remotely
record different tracks

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:42.290
and combine them into kind
of a cohesive a capella

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:45.870
product, which included
both audio and video.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:47.660
And one of the things
I think is really

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:49.160
cool about our
production is that it

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:50.870
was entirely self-contained.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:53.300
We didn't hire anyone to do
any audio editing or video

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:53.930
editing for us.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.690
And a lot of us didn't know
that much about this beforehand.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:06:00.890
But we were able to learn
throughout the pandemic

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:03.140
and eventually create
something that we're

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:05.240
pretty proud of at the end.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.390
So I guess first of all,
how this actually ended up

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:11.600
going down is we start
with an arrangement, which

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:15.368
is the center of an
a capella production.

00:06:15.368 --> 00:06:16.910
And an arrangement
is basically where

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:22.250
we, an arrangement is basically
where someone writes down

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:23.750
all the notes that
everyone is going

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:27.350
to sing, including vocal
percussion, beatboxing,

00:06:27.350 --> 00:06:28.820
and just all the
different rhythms,

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.460
all the different syllables
that people are going to sing.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:32.120
The solo.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:33.500
All the harmonies.

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:37.310
And we have that all down on
a couple of sheets of paper.

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:39.990
And we send that out
to everyone normally.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.980
And what we do in
this remote time

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:47.997
is we transformed that paper
sheet into a digital file.

00:06:47.997 --> 00:06:49.580
And then we sent
that out to everyone.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.660
So a digital audio file,
which contained basically

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:56.000
how we envisioned
the song being sung.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.910
And then everyone
listened to that recording

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:02.120
and then recorded themselves
singing their part.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:05.240
So looking at the sheet music
and listening to the reference

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:08.600
track, they would
record their part.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.720
And everyone would upload it
to a central Google Drive file.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.300
Then the person who's
mixing the audio, so mixing

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.730
is a term just in
audio production

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:20.450
meaning just to combine all
the different audio tracks

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:26.330
and make sure all the
volume levels are right,

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:28.850
apply different
effects, do things

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:33.260
like modify the frequencies to
make the sounds a little bit

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:34.093
different.

00:07:34.093 --> 00:07:35.510
And I guess I'll
show a little bit

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:37.310
about what that mixing
process looked like.

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:40.460
I'm going to share
my screen right now.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:44.120
But basically, if you look here,
here is one of our projects.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:46.300
I think this was actually
our most involved project.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:51.550
But we have, so everyone
filmed two tracks.

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:53.020
A left track and a right track.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.520
Because that creates
a greater sense

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:57.322
of space for the listener.

00:07:57.322 --> 00:07:59.530
And then, we would combine
all these tracks the other

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:03.070
in the different voice parts.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:04.810
And then we did
some timing issues

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:07.930
because, in person,
it's easy to get

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:09.370
a sense of where everyone is.

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:11.638
Sometimes if someone
rushes at one part,

00:08:11.638 --> 00:08:13.930
the whole group is able to
kind of compensate for that.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:15.610
But because we're
recording remotely,

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:19.780
it's harder to anticipate
how others are going to sing.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:22.420
And sometimes there's a
need for timing correction

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:23.980
or also pitch correction.

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:26.560
But we did all of
that within this file.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:29.560
And then we also recorded
some extra parts.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:32.860
For example, there's
a clapping section

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:34.390
towards the end of the song.

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:37.720
And I actually just recorded
myself clapping 10 times

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:39.558
into my microphone right here.

00:08:39.558 --> 00:08:41.350
So you can see it on
the screen right here.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.450
Just a bunch of
recordings of me clapping

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:44.620
at slightly different times.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:47.710
And it creates the illusion
that there's a chorus clapping.

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:48.880
But yeah.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:52.330
We were able to combine
all these different tracks

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:53.575
and apply certain effects.

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:58.730
Some examples of
effects are reverb,

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:02.930
which creates a sense that
you're in a larger concert

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:04.520
hall or something,
because there are

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:07.280
different copies
of the sound coming

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.830
at later times or
different reverberations.

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:12.710
There's an effect
called EQ, which

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:15.710
is where you modify the
different frequencies that

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:17.252
are within a sound file.

00:09:17.252 --> 00:09:18.710
And you can emphasize
certain parts

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:22.880
of the sound to create
a really different feel.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.350
And yeah.

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:25.850
That was about it
for the recording.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:29.330
And then we had everyone also
recorded videos alongside.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:31.700
And we combined those
videos together.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:35.000
And I think I'm going to
screen share real quick what

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.290
that ended up looking like.

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:40.000
[SINGING]

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.156
Is the audio playing?

00:09:42.156 --> 00:09:43.620
OK.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:45.078
[MUSIC - PARAMORE,
"AIN'T IT FUN?"]

00:09:45.078 --> 00:09:49.152
What are you gonna do when the
world don't orbit around you?

00:09:53.096 --> 00:09:57.680
So what are you gonna do when
the world don't orbit around

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:58.180
you?

00:10:01.714 --> 00:10:08.162
Ain't it fun livin'
in the real world?

00:10:11.634 --> 00:10:16.594
Ain't it good seein' all alone?

00:10:21.058 --> 00:10:24.034
Ain't it fun?

00:10:24.034 --> 00:10:27.952
You might be the one
who's running things.

00:10:27.952 --> 00:10:30.280
Well, you can make
anybody's [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:32.466
and get what you want.

00:10:32.466 --> 00:10:39.810
See, it's easy to ignore trouble
when you're living in a bubble.

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:45.740
So what are you gonna do
when the world orbit--

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:46.790
So yeah.

00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:50.630
That was what our final
product ended up looking like.

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:55.165
We ended up having, I think,
seven, six or seven songs

00:10:55.165 --> 00:10:56.540
that we created
for the semester.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:58.730
And we streamed it on Twitch.

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:00.980
And it ended up being
pretty successful.

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:03.230
We were pretty happy
with how it turned out.

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:05.430
So I think that's
about all I have.

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:06.810
Thank you for having us.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:09.560
And I'll turn it over
to the next group.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:10.340
Perfect.

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:11.460
Thank you so much.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:12.560
That was really exciting.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.530
And next, I'm going to invite
Justin from SyncopAsian

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:18.650
to get up on the
figurative stage.

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:19.160
Let's go.

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:24.030
Hello, everyone.

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:25.030
Thank you for having me.

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:26.550
So yeah.

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:29.380
As I've have been introduced,
my name is Justin.

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:31.930
I'm a third year
undergraduate at MIT.

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:35.800
And I'm here on behalf of the
a capella group SyncopAsian.

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:40.900
So we are, of course, in
a very similar situation

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:44.380
to Logs and to all the other
a capella groups on campus

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:47.410
in that, this past
semester, we've

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:50.620
been unable to perform
a concert live.

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:53.110
And so, we're struggling
with what should we

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:56.480
do in terms of producing
music over the semester

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:59.870
and also over the
following summer.

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:03.400
So we decided to take
a similar approach.

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:05.890
And we considered
putting together

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:07.910
some recording projects.

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:10.990
And so, we had a
number of arrangements

00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:12.970
which we were considering.

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:16.560
One of the biggest problems, as
has been touched on by Junhee

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:20.840
and by Ryan, is the
difficulty of rehearsing

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:24.800
in a super efficient
way while virtual.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:30.430
So we decided to focus our
efforts mostly on the recording

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:31.280
aspect of it.

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:34.060
And so, we looked
at the list of songs

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:38.380
that we have already performed
and haven't gotten around

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:41.380
to recording yet or ones
that we've been working on

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:46.540
but haven't been able
to fully bring to life

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:51.080
because we all got booted
off campus and such.

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:51.760
So yeah.

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:54.280
So we decided to
take that approach.

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:59.620
We recorded all our
audio and video virtually

00:12:59.620 --> 00:13:03.340
and pieced that
together from home,

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:05.990
had a number of people
working on that.

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:12.520
So just to share some
details of how that worked.

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:16.030
I personally was on the
production committee,

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:20.050
so to speak, for Synco
this past semester.

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:23.080
And I, in particular,
handled the audio editing

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:26.650
for our projects
that we put together.

00:13:26.650 --> 00:13:31.270
So what that entailed is
a lot of the same as what

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:33.590
Ryan showed earlier.

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:36.340
So it involved the mixing.

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:41.890
It involved retrieving
everyone's audio files,

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:47.560
balancing them properly, doing
pitch and timing corrections.

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:51.980
So one of the quick
learnings I had from that

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:56.450
is, one, is that I have a
newfound respect for people who

00:13:56.450 --> 00:14:00.955
do it professionally, having
to do that all myself,

00:14:00.955 --> 00:14:02.330
realizing that
there's a lot that

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:04.940
can go into making
something really sound good

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:08.300
and really come through
as a powerful mix

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:12.290
that you might hear on a
professional recording.

00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:14.750
But it was also a
really cool experience.

00:14:14.750 --> 00:14:19.260
Because I had almost no
knowledge about this going in.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:22.243
And so, just learning how
to do this from scratch,

00:14:22.243 --> 00:14:23.660
fiddling around
with the software,

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:27.175
fiddling around with things
I could find on Google.

00:14:27.175 --> 00:14:28.550
And if anything,
it's a testament

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:32.720
to just diving in and trying it.

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:33.780
Tossing things together.

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:34.280
Mixing it.

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:39.730
Key takeaway, I think,
for me is that there

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:43.640
is a lot more than just tossing
everything together and trying

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:45.590
to line every up
so it sounds good.

00:14:45.590 --> 00:14:48.170
Ryan showed earlier a
lot of the work that

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:51.680
goes into balancing things
and making sure that pitch

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:53.480
and timings all match up.

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:56.030
There's also a lot you can do
just with individual tracks.

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:01.160
The number of softwares, even
something free like Audacity,

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:04.100
you can do things such
as noise removals.

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:06.380
You can compress
your audio tracks.

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:11.600
You can change the EQ so you
can make certain tracks open up

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:14.100
a little more, make them
sound a little less muffled,

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:17.150
boost the bass if you are
on track that requires

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:20.390
a bit more of that,
whether it's a vocal bass

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:22.730
or vocal percussion.

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:25.580
And so, just getting a
sense of how that works,

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:28.640
balancing those properly,
so that every track really

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:33.250
comes through it the way you
want it to in the final mix.

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:38.510
I think it's something that
there's no fixed handbook to.

00:15:38.510 --> 00:15:40.520
And it's something that
you really get just

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:42.620
by fiddling around with it.

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:44.690
I think it's the
biggest takeaway.

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:51.370
Developing just trial and
error, just figuring out what

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:52.120
works the best.

00:15:56.227 --> 00:15:58.560
I don't want to dive too much
into the technical details

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:01.290
of how I did the audio mixing.

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:03.470
On the video end, we
actually had a lot of fun

00:16:03.470 --> 00:16:07.710
with this video for this
one project in particular.

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:09.370
Because, let me share my screen.

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:15.880
Is it visible to everyone?

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:16.430
Yeah.

00:16:16.430 --> 00:16:20.790
So we had a lot of fun with
this one particular project.

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:24.675
So this is something that
we had performed last fall.

00:16:24.675 --> 00:16:26.300
And so, we performed
it at our concert.

00:16:26.300 --> 00:16:27.675
And we were planning
on recording

00:16:27.675 --> 00:16:30.890
the song in a studio for
it to get professionally

00:16:30.890 --> 00:16:31.520
mixed and such.

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:35.750
But we didn't get around to
doing that because we all

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:37.080
had to leave campus.

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:40.460
And so, we considered, well,
this is a nice hype song.

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:44.720
Anyone who's familiar
with K Pop is

00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:48.440
likely to know the song
because SyncopAsian,

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:53.280
being the East Asian interest
a capella group on campus,

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:57.350
one of the big genres we
sing is this sort of K Pop.

00:16:57.350 --> 00:17:02.045
And so, we thought, OK this song
by BTS, it's a nice hype song.

00:17:02.045 --> 00:17:03.170
So we decided to record it.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:05.700
But we thought, how could
we kind of make this,

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:08.520
give it our own little twist?

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:12.079
And we saw all
sorts of a capella

00:17:12.079 --> 00:17:15.500
covers going around making
the rounds on Facebook

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:18.829
with different groups all over
the country, around the world.

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:23.010
And a Zoom themed cover
seemed to be fairly common.

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:26.300
So we really decided to kind
of hone in on that idea.

00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:30.730
Just like people from the
Logs mentioned earlier,

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:32.900
everyone just
recorded themselves

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:36.800
singing along with their
video and sent it over

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:42.200
to our video editor, Daniel
Lu, for this project.

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:44.460
And we kind of went
with the Zoom theme.

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:50.180
So we added a lot of these
little quirks across the board.

00:17:50.180 --> 00:17:55.382
Kind of just this little
chat feature, the Zoom tiles.

00:17:55.382 --> 00:17:58.280
[KPOP SINGING]

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:06.040
Insider secret.

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:07.930
The reason we had the
videos flicker on one

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:12.490
by one at the start is because
some people's videos started

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:14.200
later than we would have liked.

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:19.120
So, hide the error with
a staggered entrance.

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:19.870
Looks cool though.

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:22.040
And it looks very Zoomy.

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:22.540
Yeah.

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:24.730
Make with it what
you can, I suppose.

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:25.230
Yeah.

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:27.603
So we had a lot of
fun on this project

00:18:27.603 --> 00:18:29.020
because there were
a lot of things

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:32.500
that we could do with
regards to giving

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:34.930
the these little quirks
that people who use

00:18:34.930 --> 00:18:37.460
Zoom are very familiar with.

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:41.000
For example, if I can
skip forward a bit--

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:42.900
[KPOP SINGING]

00:18:54.216 --> 00:18:55.695
I've waited all my life.

00:18:55.695 --> 00:18:58.653
[SINGING IN KOREAN]

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:05.200
My internet's not fantastic.

00:19:08.665 --> 00:19:11.140
[KPOP MUSIC]

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:27.060
All right.

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:29.490
It looks like we're having a
bit of an internet connection

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:30.750
difficulty with Justin.

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:31.420
Are you back?

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:31.920
Yep.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:32.640
I'm back.

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:33.290
Sorry, my internet is--

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:33.790
All right.

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:34.475
Excellent.

00:19:34.475 --> 00:19:37.080
Potentially a little
questionable right now.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:37.920
Please don't worry.

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:39.347
We've got about
two more minutes.

00:19:39.347 --> 00:19:41.430
So if you want to wrap it
up, that would be great.

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:43.110
That was really excellent video.

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:43.610
Yeah.

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:44.110
Sure.

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:46.050
So yeah.

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:49.230
One thing I didn't
quite get to show off

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:52.550
there, which I won't make
an attempt right now.

00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:55.070
Just because my internet
might die again.

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:58.008
We did little things where
we added some quirks.

00:19:58.008 --> 00:19:59.050
There's a second soloist.

00:19:59.050 --> 00:20:02.960
So when they switched over,
they raised their hand over Zoom

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:04.952
to start soloing.

00:20:04.952 --> 00:20:07.160
We added little Zoom emojis
when the vocal percussion

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:08.918
did fancy things.

00:20:08.918 --> 00:20:11.210
And so, this was really nice
because it was also really

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:13.490
a cool collaborative effort.

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:16.232
Even though one person was
spearheading the video editing,

00:20:16.232 --> 00:20:17.690
there was something
a lot of people

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:20.660
could give feedback on in
designing the storyboard,

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:22.670
so to speak, for
the whole video.

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:25.210
Deciding what little features
we could add in, where

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:27.450
transitions should happen.

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:30.200
And so, this was a
really cool way for us

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:33.680
to still be able to
work collaboratively

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:37.670
on a project that wasn't
just people recording,

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:40.400
sending it to people
to do all the work.

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:43.200
Even past that, people could
still contribute their ideas.

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:46.970
Find ways to give their
feedback on the way

00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:51.200
the audio is coming along, on
what we could do for the video.

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:54.570
We had a number of
iterations of both the audio,

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:57.110
where we sent it to
the group and made

00:20:57.110 --> 00:20:59.510
sure everyone was
really on the same page

00:20:59.510 --> 00:21:01.658
with where this
project was going.

00:21:01.658 --> 00:21:02.200
And so, yeah.

00:21:02.200 --> 00:21:06.100
I think overall I'd say that
doing this sort of recording

00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:11.080
project, we put together
some other recordings,

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:13.452
as well, over this
semester and the summer.

00:21:13.452 --> 00:21:15.160
This is, I think, the
biggest one we did,

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:16.540
the one I just showed you.

00:21:16.540 --> 00:21:18.040
But these sorts of
project, I think,

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:22.100
were a really cool way to stay
connected over the semester

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:25.750
and over the summer, to keep
producing music together,

00:21:25.750 --> 00:21:30.350
to really feel like everyone's
getting involved in this

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:34.310
and really still
contributing to the group

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:36.410
and to what we like to do.

00:21:36.410 --> 00:21:38.680
Make music for the community.

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:39.340
So yeah.

00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:41.170
It's been an awesome experience.

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:43.360
And we're definitely
going to continue

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:46.730
recording and producing
things this semester.

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:47.230
Excellent.

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:48.040
Thank you so much.

00:21:48.040 --> 00:21:49.720
If you want, we
would love for you

00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:52.210
to share some of the
other recording projects

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:53.830
that you've done.

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:55.540
Probably in the chat
would be the best.

00:21:55.540 --> 00:21:57.310
But we can also
take those via email

00:21:57.310 --> 00:22:00.370
and send them out
to people, as well.

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:00.870
All right.

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:03.600
And with that, we're going to
go to our last presentation.

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:05.520
And that is Professor
Ian Hattwick.

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:07.540
Take it away.

00:22:07.540 --> 00:22:08.040
Hi.

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:09.900
So I'm Ian Hattwick.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:13.020
I'm a Professor at MIT in the
Music Technology Department

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:14.830
in Music and Theater Arts.

00:22:14.830 --> 00:22:17.130
And I'm also the
Director of Family, which

00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:19.013
is that MIT laptop ensemble.

00:22:19.013 --> 00:22:20.430
So I thought I'd
talk a little bit

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:25.020
about how Family has approached
performing and rehearsing

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:26.720
remotely.

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:28.350
Sort of how last
semester went and what

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:29.370
we're doing this semester.

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:30.453
And it's pretty different.

00:22:30.453 --> 00:22:34.380
It was really great to hear
the earlier two presentations.

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:36.120
Obviously, the a
capella community at MIT

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:36.960
is really strong.

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:39.185
And they've done lots of
things over the years.

00:22:39.185 --> 00:22:40.560
And I've actually,
in my classes,

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:43.500
had quite a few a capella
students learn about recording.

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:47.700
So I was actually
curious to see how

00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:50.430
sort of the existing knowledge
within the a capella community

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:54.270
would feed into the ability
to deal with this transition

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:56.940
in doing these remote
recordings, which were

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:58.720
both really super successful.

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:00.080
So congratulations.

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:02.580
And good job on that.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:04.580
But what we ended up
doing is really different.

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:07.830
And actually, we were
the only ensemble at MIT

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:12.420
that I know of that can kept
our performance schedule exactly

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:16.440
the same as it was before the
transition to being remote

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:18.210
as it was after.

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:21.570
So we were able to do that
because the laptop ensemble

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:24.490
already focuses on
network music performance.

00:23:24.490 --> 00:23:27.210
And I guess with that I should
maybe mentioned a little bit

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:29.043
about what the laptop
ensemble is all about,

00:23:29.043 --> 00:23:30.630
what a laptop ensemble is.

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:33.570
So we're an ensemble,
a small group.

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:36.330
Generally around six to
seven to eight people

00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:38.220
who perform together
using laptops

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:41.370
as our primary sound
source and interface.

00:23:41.370 --> 00:23:43.050
Sometimes, we'll
use MIDI controllers

00:23:43.050 --> 00:23:45.080
or other kinds of hardware.

00:23:45.080 --> 00:23:48.240
But most of the time, all of the
sound that we're working with

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:51.862
is generated on a laptop
and then either sent out

00:23:51.862 --> 00:23:53.070
and played through a speaker.

00:23:53.070 --> 00:23:56.040
Or when we're
playing remotely, we

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:58.800
would send it out
over Zoom or something

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:02.670
like that or YouTube
streaming for concerts.

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:05.700
So a lot of the focus on
this is, what does a laptop

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.078
bring to an ensemble context?

00:24:07.078 --> 00:24:09.120
And how do you perform
electronic music together?

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:11.940
And it's not at all
obvious for a lot of people

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:13.915
how that would play out.

00:24:13.915 --> 00:24:16.290
Electronic music, the way that
we see it most of the time

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:20.130
is actually a solo or a duo
at the most working together.

00:24:20.130 --> 00:24:22.665
We see DJs, we see solo artists.

00:24:22.665 --> 00:24:24.540
And this idea of having
an ensemble of people

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:28.553
communicating is not
that common nowadays.

00:24:28.553 --> 00:24:29.970
And the neat thing
about it is, it

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:35.640
gives new ways of collaborating
that really are still,

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:39.270
we're in there in the early days
of exploring how this can work.

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:42.810
So before even the switch
to being remote happened,

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:44.310
we were already
doing network music.

00:24:44.310 --> 00:24:47.070
We're experimenting with
creating information

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:49.490
on my laptop and sending that
information to your laptop.

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:51.073
And then you can
take that information

00:24:51.073 --> 00:24:52.357
and use it to generate music.

00:24:52.357 --> 00:24:53.940
And then you can
send your information

00:24:53.940 --> 00:24:54.960
to somebody else's laptop.

00:24:54.960 --> 00:24:57.127
And they can use that
information to generate music.

00:24:57.127 --> 00:24:59.405
And then you're hearing all
of that at the same time.

00:24:59.405 --> 00:25:01.530
So I thought maybe I'd play
a couple of videos just

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:06.180
to orient you about what our
normal performance practice is

00:25:06.180 --> 00:25:06.810
like.

00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:08.790
And then, just play a
couple of videos also

00:25:08.790 --> 00:25:10.170
of what happened last semester.

00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:12.140
And I can talk about
how that played out

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:15.960
and how we set up the system
to be able to do that.

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:27.373
So that being said,
this is a recap of--

00:25:27.373 --> 00:25:29.040
of course, the Zoom
end is blocking me--

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:36.756
of our fall 2019 season.

00:25:36.756 --> 00:25:40.214
[ELECTRONIC MUSIC PLAYING]

00:26:11.860 --> 00:26:14.850
So one of the things that you're
not seeing in this right now

00:26:14.850 --> 00:26:17.220
is that every performer has
their own speaker that's

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:18.292
right next to them.

00:26:18.292 --> 00:26:19.875
So the idea is that,
it's just like it

00:26:19.875 --> 00:26:20.880
would be in a music ensemble.

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:22.260
When somebody is making
sound, that sound

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:24.240
is coming from that
performer's location.

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:26.250
And when you're in the
room with everyone,

00:26:26.250 --> 00:26:28.463
the sound is distributed
according to the ensemble.

00:26:28.463 --> 00:26:30.130
So you can tell what
everybody is doing.

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:33.060
And there's really a sense of
spatial identity and spatial

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:35.370
resonance in the room.

00:26:35.370 --> 00:26:37.890
And we would do all different
kinds of formations.

00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:39.810
We would stand in small circles.

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:42.330
We would sit down on
the floor by the piano.

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:44.880
We would sit amongst
the audience sometimes.

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:47.010
We would go and sit
and stand all the way

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:49.170
around the room
in a giant circle.

00:26:49.170 --> 00:26:52.480
And even walk
around a little bit.

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:55.290
So really, we'd play
around with space a lot,

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:57.180
play around with
spatialization a lot,

00:26:57.180 --> 00:27:00.130
really concerned with this idea
of sharing musical materials,

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:01.480
things like that.

00:27:01.480 --> 00:27:04.920
So when it came time
to move remotely,

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:09.173
we were able to take that same
approach when we went online.

00:27:09.173 --> 00:27:10.590
And one of the
reasons why we were

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:12.060
able to do that
so successfully is

00:27:12.060 --> 00:27:14.620
that we're already sharing
information over networks.

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:15.120
Right?

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:17.040
That's part of our
normal practice.

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:19.680
So the idea that we would share
information over the internet

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:24.048
remotely across, you know,
across the US and in Taiwan,

00:27:24.048 --> 00:27:25.590
it was really easy
for us to do that.

00:27:25.590 --> 00:27:28.020
But we did have to create
some new software that

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:30.833
would generate all of the
sound that you would hear.

00:27:30.833 --> 00:27:32.250
Everybody's laptop
would basically

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:33.375
generate all of the sounds.

00:27:33.375 --> 00:27:35.055
So everybody would
be hearing the sound

00:27:35.055 --> 00:27:36.680
from everybody's
sound on their laptop.

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:38.160
And then everybody would
be sending control messages

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:39.040
to everybody else.

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:41.370
It was a giant network of
people sending information

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:43.037
back and forth.

00:27:43.037 --> 00:27:45.120
One of the interesting
things about last semester,

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:46.912
too, that was slightly
different is that we

00:27:46.912 --> 00:27:48.360
were using a controller.

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:52.050
So this piece, "Cursors,"
is using a launchpad grid

00:27:52.050 --> 00:27:54.270
controller as our input device.

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:59.910
And we had a visualization
for this piece where everybody

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:02.040
had control over
one little chunk

00:28:02.040 --> 00:28:03.870
of this circular sequence.

00:28:03.870 --> 00:28:07.050
And they could control the notes
and placements on the sequence

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:08.792
and interact both
with placing events

00:28:08.792 --> 00:28:10.500
and also interact with
this cursor, which

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:11.793
is triggering events.

00:28:11.793 --> 00:28:13.710
This is a little bit of
this piece, "Cursors."

00:28:13.710 --> 00:28:16.686
[ELECTRONIC MUSIC PLAYING]

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:19.430
So when we perform this,
this is actually a video

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:22.850
that I produced
for an MTA event.

00:29:22.850 --> 00:29:24.650
But this is also exactly
how we perform it.

00:29:24.650 --> 00:29:26.600
All of these videos
of these performances

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:28.410
were taking place
at the same time.

00:29:28.410 --> 00:29:29.975
So this was a real
time performance

00:29:29.975 --> 00:29:31.350
in all these
different locations.

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:33.100
We just had everybody
sort of use a webcam

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:34.670
to record their local video.

00:29:34.670 --> 00:29:37.570
And then, we edited it together
into a nice presentation.

00:29:37.570 --> 00:29:40.760
But we would do the
same thing for concerts.

00:29:40.760 --> 00:29:46.550
So this is a concert
that we did over Zoom.

00:29:46.550 --> 00:29:48.500
And we actually, the
way we did it is we

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:50.600
would have a big Zoom
room everybody was in.

00:29:50.600 --> 00:29:53.450
And then, we were streaming
the audio and the video

00:29:53.450 --> 00:29:55.640
from my laptop through
YouTube Studio.

00:29:55.640 --> 00:29:59.042
And actually we were streaming
to different people's,

00:29:59.042 --> 00:30:01.430
different people would
share their screens.

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:03.170
And we would stream
from one laptop

00:30:03.170 --> 00:30:06.360
to YouTube streaming services.

00:30:06.360 --> 00:30:08.090
So this is another
concern that we did,

00:30:08.090 --> 00:30:10.820
another live concert that
was played synchronously.

00:30:10.820 --> 00:30:13.320
[ELECTRONIC MUSIC PLAYING]

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:41.040
And I think we're getting
basically mono sound here.

00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:43.998
Zoom does not play very
well with stereo sound.

00:30:43.998 --> 00:30:45.540
But one component
that you're missing

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:49.800
is we're also able to
implement a 360 degree spatial

00:30:49.800 --> 00:30:50.943
audio algorithm.

00:30:50.943 --> 00:30:52.860
So you could actually
hear each person's sound

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:54.610
coming from a different
location in space.

00:30:54.610 --> 00:30:57.330
You're not getting that right
now because Zoom does not

00:30:57.330 --> 00:31:01.275
support stereo audio
natively, anyway.

00:31:05.222 --> 00:31:07.680
So that was something that we
were able to do, like I said,

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:09.330
largely because we
were already working

00:31:09.330 --> 00:31:12.900
with network performances.

00:31:12.900 --> 00:31:21.483
And it enabled us to translate
that into this virtual setting.

00:31:21.483 --> 00:31:22.900
So this semester--
and the reason,

00:31:22.900 --> 00:31:24.240
one reason I wanted to
show that last video--

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:26.520
is, this semester, we're
doing entirely online.

00:31:26.520 --> 00:31:28.020
Some of the other
semesters at MIT--

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:30.430
sorry, some of the
other ensembles at MIT

00:31:30.430 --> 00:31:33.940
are trying to bring in
some synchronous and even

00:31:33.940 --> 00:31:36.610
some in-person rehearsal
and performance.

00:31:36.610 --> 00:31:40.270
And I decided that, since
working virtually works really

00:31:40.270 --> 00:31:42.460
well for us, it's actually
pretty comfortable based

00:31:42.460 --> 00:31:45.240
on the way that we work, we
would stay entirely virtual

00:31:45.240 --> 00:31:46.810
so everybody has
the same experience.

00:31:46.810 --> 00:31:50.140
There's no concern about whether
people are on campus or not.

00:31:50.140 --> 00:31:52.600
And we're also exploring
something else, which

00:31:52.600 --> 00:31:54.610
is a live coding performance.

00:31:54.610 --> 00:31:56.640
So live coding is
this situation where

00:31:56.640 --> 00:31:59.350
people are typing code in
real time, executing it,

00:31:59.350 --> 00:32:01.060
and then it generates sound.

00:32:01.060 --> 00:32:03.130
So just to give you
a quick sense of what

00:32:03.130 --> 00:32:08.490
live coding looks like, this
is our live coding environment

00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:09.570
we're using.

00:32:09.570 --> 00:32:12.000
We're actually
writing code and then

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:14.867
executing it to generate sound.

00:32:14.867 --> 00:32:16.200
I'm not getting sound right now.

00:32:16.200 --> 00:32:18.022
[FEEDBACK]

00:32:18.022 --> 00:32:19.980
Just because it's not
playing nicely with Zoom.

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:25.410
But that's what we're going
to be doing this semester.

00:32:25.410 --> 00:32:28.170
So we've got a really different
perspective than SyncopAsian

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:30.210
and Logarhythms and a lot
of the other ensembles

00:32:30.210 --> 00:32:33.323
at MIT because this system
is very natural for us.

00:32:33.323 --> 00:32:34.740
So maybe I'll wrap
up right there.

00:32:34.740 --> 00:32:38.490
I do have a lot of
questions for the other two

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:40.850
presenters and the MIT
community generally.

00:32:40.850 --> 00:32:42.600
There are a lot of
really interesting ways

00:32:42.600 --> 00:32:45.120
of approaching dealing with
this virtual music performance.

00:32:45.120 --> 00:32:46.530
But I'll wrap up my section.

00:32:46.530 --> 00:32:49.150
And we can move onto the Q&amp;A
or have a little discussion.

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:52.150
You've just basically
handled my transition for me.

00:32:52.150 --> 00:32:54.240
I was about to thank
all of you and suggest

00:32:54.240 --> 00:32:57.300
that we move into
some Q&amp;A. If anybody

00:32:57.300 --> 00:33:00.150
has any questions, please feel
free to pop them in the chat

00:33:00.150 --> 00:33:02.070
or to use the Raise
Hand function.

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:05.820
And I guess I will start about
how you're all planning to--

00:33:05.820 --> 00:33:08.610
I mean, Ian touched on
this-- but how is everybody

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:12.920
planning to continue rehearsing
and producing music virtually?

00:33:12.920 --> 00:33:13.920
I open up the floor.

00:33:18.250 --> 00:33:22.280
For us, our approach is very
similar to this last semester.

00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:25.310
So we're going to
continue producing videos

00:33:25.310 --> 00:33:26.870
and trying to hold
another concert

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:28.340
at the end of this semester.

00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:31.310
I think the main
difference this semester

00:33:31.310 --> 00:33:34.640
will be that what we're
actually trying to rehearse.

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:40.370
So of course, there is still not
going to be any audio syncing

00:33:40.370 --> 00:33:41.332
through Zoom.

00:33:41.332 --> 00:33:43.040
And there are going
to be latency delays.

00:33:43.040 --> 00:33:46.280
But what we're trying to do
is just share computer sound,

00:33:46.280 --> 00:33:48.200
sing along to ourselves,
kind of emulate

00:33:48.200 --> 00:33:52.190
the concept of rehearsal
without the reactiveness.

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:54.020
So that's going to
be one way we're

00:33:54.020 --> 00:33:57.300
going to try to enhance
the music learning process,

00:33:57.300 --> 00:34:01.580
especially since we're doing all
new arrangements this semester.

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:03.480
Does anyone else going
to say something?

00:34:03.480 --> 00:34:04.860
I have some questions, actually.

00:34:04.860 --> 00:34:06.990
If anybody has any
interest or time,

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:10.800
I know it's a big subject,
but I'm a musician myself.

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:12.810
And we've been struggling
to come up with ideas

00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:15.070
for using low latency programs.

00:34:15.070 --> 00:34:17.100
So if anybody has
any ideas about that,

00:34:17.100 --> 00:34:20.110
I'd be really curious
to hear about it.

00:34:20.110 --> 00:34:24.980
I could probably chime in,
unless somebody else wants to--

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:26.800
Go ahead, go ahead.

00:34:26.800 --> 00:34:28.239
So there's a lot
of different ways

00:34:28.239 --> 00:34:30.650
that people haven't tried
to do low latency audio.

00:34:30.650 --> 00:34:32.739
There's actually
a move right now,

00:34:32.739 --> 00:34:36.100
I think it's called a LLAMIT,
Low Latency Audio at MIT.

00:34:36.100 --> 00:34:38.100
Professor Aaron [INAUDIBLE]
is another professor

00:34:38.100 --> 00:34:43.054
in the Music Tech Department at
MTA, has been organizing this.

00:34:43.054 --> 00:34:44.679
So there has been a
community of people

00:34:44.679 --> 00:34:46.409
who have been networking
music for a long time.

00:34:46.409 --> 00:34:48.117
And they use this tool
called Jack Tripp.

00:34:48.117 --> 00:34:53.590
It's an open source, low
latency audio network connection

00:34:53.590 --> 00:34:55.873
where you can stream audio
from multiple locations

00:34:55.873 --> 00:34:57.040
to other multiple locations.

00:34:57.040 --> 00:34:59.710
And you can do this kind
of multi-site real time

00:34:59.710 --> 00:35:00.760
performance.

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:02.710
But it's really
challenging to do this

00:35:02.710 --> 00:35:04.090
in a way that is effective.

00:35:04.090 --> 00:35:06.070
We all know that
Zoom doesn't work.

00:35:06.070 --> 00:35:07.120
Right?

00:35:07.120 --> 00:35:08.650
And the reason why
is because Zoom

00:35:08.650 --> 00:35:12.130
has all this latency, which is
built in to buffer the audio.

00:35:12.130 --> 00:35:14.654
And so, Jack Tripp is
trying to implement

00:35:14.654 --> 00:35:16.446
a more efficient network
where you can sort

00:35:16.446 --> 00:35:17.950
of share audio in real time.

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:19.990
But you're always going to be
limited by a couple of things.

00:35:19.990 --> 00:35:21.190
You're limited, first
of all, by the speed

00:35:21.190 --> 00:35:23.590
of light, which is
actually not inconsiderable

00:35:23.590 --> 00:35:26.980
when you're dealing with
intercontinental audio, which

00:35:26.980 --> 00:35:29.560
can add many milliseconds
of a delay to the audio.

00:35:29.560 --> 00:35:32.020
And then also, signals
going through the internet.

00:35:32.020 --> 00:35:35.080
The internet is not designed for
real time network transmission.

00:35:35.080 --> 00:35:36.490
It's designed to buffer.

00:35:36.490 --> 00:35:39.130
And it's designed to
get information there

00:35:39.130 --> 00:35:42.760
reliably rather than in time.

00:35:42.760 --> 00:35:45.050
So dealing with these
things can be really tricky.

00:35:45.050 --> 00:35:47.650
I think that what
Aaron has found--

00:35:47.650 --> 00:35:49.930
well, if you have a really
robust internet network,

00:35:49.930 --> 00:35:52.900
like MIT does, like an
academic university does,

00:35:52.900 --> 00:35:55.390
you can leverage that
academic network,

00:35:55.390 --> 00:35:59.692
which is going to not have many
of the same latency problems

00:35:59.692 --> 00:36:01.150
that commercial
internet will have.

00:36:01.150 --> 00:36:03.490
Like our home internet will
have much worse latency

00:36:03.490 --> 00:36:06.070
than a university
infrastructure internet.

00:36:06.070 --> 00:36:08.170
So if everybody is
at a university,

00:36:08.170 --> 00:36:11.100
then using something like Jack
Tripp to do low latency audio

00:36:11.100 --> 00:36:12.900
is actually pretty feasible.

00:36:12.900 --> 00:36:15.460
And again, mostly feasible
within like a 400 or 500

00:36:15.460 --> 00:36:16.150
mile range.

00:36:16.150 --> 00:36:18.192
Because once you get out
of the 400 or 500 miles,

00:36:18.192 --> 00:36:21.590
the speed of light becomes
a factor in terms of delay.

00:36:21.590 --> 00:36:23.947
So that, I think, is
sort of the situation

00:36:23.947 --> 00:36:25.030
that's going on with that.

00:36:25.030 --> 00:36:27.257
If you're trying to do
low latency audio locally,

00:36:27.257 --> 00:36:28.090
it's not impossible.

00:36:28.090 --> 00:36:32.320
But also to set these things
up is not technically trivial.

00:36:32.320 --> 00:36:34.810
So it takes a lot
of trial and error

00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:37.430
and a lot of [INAUDIBLE]
figuring this stuff out.

00:36:37.430 --> 00:36:39.500
So possible, but not easy.

00:36:39.500 --> 00:36:40.990
Most people aren't doing it.

00:36:40.990 --> 00:36:43.490
If it was easy, people would
be doing it all over the place.

00:36:43.490 --> 00:36:46.150
And it's hard enough that
most people are not doing it.

00:36:46.150 --> 00:36:50.140
Has anybody here had any
experience with that?

00:36:50.140 --> 00:36:52.510
I actually just wanted to
tack something onto that.

00:36:52.510 --> 00:36:55.080
So I haven't gotten
experience yet.

00:36:55.080 --> 00:36:57.940
But I will be, because I am
also part of the Chamber Music

00:36:57.940 --> 00:36:59.500
program at Emerson.

00:36:59.500 --> 00:37:03.130
So I have been discussing
with the people

00:37:03.130 --> 00:37:06.060
there about working with
this low latency technology.

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:10.080
So I've seen a lot of what's
been discussed about it.

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:12.760
And regarding
SyncopAsian specifically,

00:37:12.760 --> 00:37:17.350
we've considered the option
of using this low latency

00:37:17.350 --> 00:37:18.420
technology.

00:37:18.420 --> 00:37:21.160
And we might for just
the occasional jam

00:37:21.160 --> 00:37:22.360
session or whatever.

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:25.630
But like Logs mentioned, we
won't be doing rehearsals

00:37:25.630 --> 00:37:28.960
in the traditional sense
of singing synchronously.

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:30.790
Because even, if we
get everything optimal,

00:37:30.790 --> 00:37:32.890
we have people on the
other side of the planet.

00:37:32.890 --> 00:37:37.060
And at a certain point,
low latency solutions

00:37:37.060 --> 00:37:39.310
just don't work.

00:37:39.310 --> 00:37:41.860
So we're definitely
limited in that sense.

00:37:41.860 --> 00:37:45.290
And so, our rehearsals
this semester--

00:37:45.290 --> 00:37:47.500
or we're labeling them
more as workshops,

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:49.300
because they're not
going to be rehearsals

00:37:49.300 --> 00:37:52.540
in the same usual sense, like
singing together and doing

00:37:52.540 --> 00:37:55.350
everything synchronously.

00:37:55.350 --> 00:37:58.240
We are still playing to
produce things similar to how

00:37:58.240 --> 00:37:59.800
we did this past semester.

00:37:59.800 --> 00:38:02.920
But unfortunately,
we still won't

00:38:02.920 --> 00:38:05.020
be able to get the same
feel of singing together.

00:38:07.540 --> 00:38:09.430
That's great to hear.

00:38:09.430 --> 00:38:11.590
I also saw that Ryan
looked like he was trying

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:12.890
to chime in at one point.

00:38:12.890 --> 00:38:14.500
So, as we're about
to wrap up, I just

00:38:14.500 --> 00:38:17.123
want to make sure that he
has a chance to come in, too.

00:38:20.065 --> 00:38:21.690
I mean, most of what
I was going to say

00:38:21.690 --> 00:38:24.570
was actually said
by Ian and Justin.

00:38:24.570 --> 00:38:26.490
I guess I'll add in
that we did end up

00:38:26.490 --> 00:38:30.840
trying a low latency solution
on a program called Jam Kazam.

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:38.460
And it was not feasible with
someone across the country.

00:38:38.460 --> 00:38:39.930
I tried doing it with--

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:41.070
I'm based in Virginia.

00:38:41.070 --> 00:38:42.700
And I tried doing it with
someone in California.

00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:44.450
And the delays were
something like a tenth

00:38:44.450 --> 00:38:47.130
of a second, which
is just a non-starter

00:38:47.130 --> 00:38:49.680
for simultaneous rehearsal.

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:51.630
I did try it with
a friend in DC.

00:38:51.630 --> 00:38:53.520
So he was about 40 miles away.

00:38:53.520 --> 00:38:56.880
And it was pretty
acceptable at that point.

00:38:56.880 --> 00:38:59.370
So I think, in the
future, especially

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:01.677
if the software is
improved, perhaps

00:39:01.677 --> 00:39:02.760
we can't do cross country.

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:05.670
But something if
everyone's in Boston

00:39:05.670 --> 00:39:08.580
but we can't meet up in person
due to social distancing

00:39:08.580 --> 00:39:11.010
requirements, we could all
rehearse together in a room.

00:39:11.010 --> 00:39:13.140
I could see that becoming
a reality at some point.

00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:15.400
Although probably not before
the end of this pandemic.

00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:20.000
All right.

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:21.830
We are coming up on
the end of the session.

00:39:21.830 --> 00:39:23.300
This has been really fantastic.

00:39:23.300 --> 00:39:25.190
If anybody has
any parting shots,

00:39:25.190 --> 00:39:26.310
I would love to hear them.

00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:30.620
But if not, I'd like to
invite you to stick around.

00:39:30.620 --> 00:39:32.995
In this room, we'll
have a 15 minute break.

00:39:32.995 --> 00:39:35.120
And there are going to be
some slideshows happening

00:39:35.120 --> 00:39:37.610
with some information
surrounding all of Arts

00:39:37.610 --> 00:39:38.240
on the Radar.

00:39:38.240 --> 00:39:40.520
And there's going to
be some nifty trivia

00:39:40.520 --> 00:39:42.040
about the arts at MIT.

00:39:42.040 --> 00:39:45.540
So if you feel like hanging
out, that would be great.

00:39:45.540 --> 00:39:48.710
And if you'd rather get up
stretch, have a drink of water,

00:39:48.710 --> 00:39:51.230
or something at
8:00 in this room,

00:39:51.230 --> 00:39:53.630
we'll be doing a
roundtable panel

00:39:53.630 --> 00:39:57.350
with some of our most actively
engaged student artists at MIT.

00:39:57.350 --> 00:40:01.820
And they'll discuss their
experiences engaging with Arts

00:40:01.820 --> 00:40:03.560
on Campus and virtually.

00:40:03.560 --> 00:40:08.510
And in the other Zoom room,
which we can send information

00:40:08.510 --> 00:40:12.260
about on the website,
there is a presentation,

00:40:12.260 --> 00:40:14.740
a Maker workshop through ACT--

00:40:14.740 --> 00:40:16.320
Arts, Culture, and Technology.

00:40:16.320 --> 00:40:21.290
So with that, I bid
you all farewell.

00:40:21.290 --> 00:40:23.962
Thank you all so much.

00:40:23.962 --> 00:40:24.670
Thanks, everyone.

